\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalize evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction as a structured generation task for LLMs and conduct a systematic comparison of three post-training paradigms across 85+ configurations. Our findings reveal that RL optimization pressure correlates with evidence-extraction decoupling severity (GRPO catastrophic, DPO gradual, RSFT none; EDCR $\rho = -0.965$), and that the cost of joint evidence production follows a non-monotonic pattern across scales (+8pp at 1.7B, $-$7.5pp at 4B, near zero at 8B). RSFT with CED scoring is the only effective route, yielding +4.92pp Ign-F1 at 4B (four-seed mean), while our 1.7B RSFT model (50.50 Ign-F1) matches 8B SFT (50.51) at a fraction of the cost. The framework extends naturally to other extraction tasks where output verifiability is critical; iterative RSFT may further reduce the evidence tax at scales where it is most severe.

